2. Related work
The evaluation of LLMs with various prompting techniques is an emerging and significant research area for both low-resource and high-resource languages. In this study, we assessed the performance of LLMs on Bengali tasks, including text classification (news and sentiment), summarization, and question answering, using three prompting techniques: Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought in six LLMs. The following subsection presents a summary of research related to these prompting techniques for both of low and high resource languages.
2.1. Zero-Shot prompting
Recently, Zero-Shot prompting in LLMs has gained significant attention from researchers due to its effectiveness in scenarios where task-specific models or knowledge are unavailable. The reasoning capabilities of Zero-Shot LLM models are evaluated in López Espejel et al. (2023), where the Zero-Shot performance of GPT-3.5, GPT-4, and BARD models is empirically assessed for the English language. Similarly, D’Asaro et al. (2024) developed a multimodal recommendation system for English using Zero-Shot prompting, combining image embeddings from CLIP and text embeddings from ALBEF.
A low-resource Turkish text classification system was developed in Çelik and Dalyan (2023) using Zero-Shot prompting on ConvBERT, achieving a maximum accuracy of 79%. Barrena et al. (2021) introduced a cross-lingual named entity disambiguation system with XLM-R, targeting low-resource Spanish alongside high-resource English and Chinese. Kabir et al. (2024) empirically evaluated the performance of Zero-Shot LLMs on Bengali tasks, including summarization, question answering, and sentiment analysis. While Zero-Shot LLMs show reasonable accuracy for many high-resource languages, their performance on low-resource tasks remains suboptimal.
2.2. Few-Shot prompting
The remarkable success of generative pre-trained transformers (GPT) has brought Few-Shot prompting in LLMs into the spotlight, particularly for low-resource NLP tasks. Cheng et al. (2024) developed a Few-Shot LLM-based NER system that achieved an F1-score of 88.7% on the CoNLL dataset and 78.3% on the FewNERD dataset using the GPT-4 model. Similarly, Song et al. (2023) introduced a Few-Shot event classification system for two English datasets, i.e., FewEvent and RAMS. Mahfuz et al. (2024) evaluated the performance of open-weight and closed-source LLMs, such as LLaMA-3 and GPT-4, alongside fine-tuned encoder–decoder models on various Bengali NLP tasks, including translation, summarization, and question answering. The results indicate that while LLMs perform well in reasoning tasks, their effectiveness in Bengali script generation is inconsistent due to challenges such as inefficient tokenization, which leads to higher computational costs and degraded performance.
A Few-Shot LLM-based system was developed for English text summarization, achieving a maximum R1 score of 0.3729, R2 score of 0.0992, and RL score of 0.2276 on the Yelp test set (Bražinskas et al., 2020). Similarly, Ram et al. (2021) introduced a Few-Shot LLM question-answering system, utilizing 100,000 annotated questions and 128 training examples as prior knowledge, achieving a maximum F1 score of 72.7% on the SQuAD dataset. Overall, Few-Shot prompting has proven to be more effective and yields better accuracy compared to Zero-Shot prompting.

