2.3. Chain-of-Thought prompting
Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting enhances the performance of LLMs by improving their reasoning capabilities, allowing them to solve complex tasks through step-by-step thinking (Wei et al., 2024). Kalyan (2024) provided a comprehensive review of GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 research, with a particular focus on recent advancements in CoT-based prompting. Additionally, Rasool et al. (2024) developed a question-answering system using CoT prompting and evaluated the performance of various LLMs. Their results show that the GPT-4 model achieved the highest accuracy of 37.39% on CogTale dataset. These studies demonstrate that CoT prompting can significantly enhance LLMs’ ability to handle tasks requiring multi-step reasoning, though the effectiveness may vary across models and tasks.
Shi et al. (2022) introduced the Multilingual Grade School Math (MGSM) benchmark, featuring 250 math problems from the GSM8K dataset translated into ten diverse languages. Their findings highlight that large language models (LLMs), particularly when utilizing CoT prompting, exhibit strong multilingual reasoning abilities, including in underrepresented languages like Bengali and Swahili. The study further demonstrates that CoT prompting not only enhances performance in mathematical problem-solving but also improves LLMs’ reasoning capabilities in non-mathematical tasks, such as commonsense reasoning and word-in-context semantic judgment. Toraman (2024) developed a low-resource Turkish downstream open-source LLMs models adaption system. They employed intrinsic evaluation for word quality assessment, instruction fine-tuning, tasks specific fine-tuning and quantinual training. Sentiment analysis and reasoning tasks using LLMs with CoT-based prompting achieved state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance in Fei et al. (2023), with a maximum accuracy of 87.45% on the restaurant dataset and 85.16% on the laptop review dataset. Overall, CoT-based prompting not only enhances the reasoning capabilities of LLMs but also helps explain the causes of hallucinated outputs and provides guidance for mitigating such issues effectively.
2.4. Differences from existing researches
Most previous research has focused on high-resource languages, such as English, using Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and CoT-based prompting for tasks like reasoning, recommendation systems, NER, question answering, and event classification (López Espejel et al., 2023, D’Asaro et al., 2024, Cheng et al., 2024, Song et al., 2023, Rasool et al., 2024). In contrast, limited research has explored low-resource languages, such as Turkish, Spanish, and Bengali, applying these prompting techniques for text classification, summarization, and question answering tasks (Çelik and Dalyan, 2023, Barrena et al., 2021, Kabir et al., 2024, Mahfuz et al., 2024). This research distinguishes itself from prior studies in the following ways:
•
This study is first to evaluate the effectiveness of Chain-of- Thought (CoT) prompting on Bengali language tasks, alongside Zero-Shot and Few-Shot techniques.
•
Focuses on multiple Bengali downstream tasks, including news classification, sentiment analysis, summarization, and question answering, offering comprehensive coverage.
•
Compares the performance of open-source models (e.g., BLOOM, LLaMA, Aya 101) with closed-source commercial models (e.g., GPT-4o, Gemini, GPT-3.5), highlighting their strengths and limitations for low-resource languages.
•
Presents empirical evidence demonstrating the performance differences between high-resource and low-resource languages, filling a crucial gap in the literature on multilingual LLMs.
These contributions offer valuable insights into the effectiveness of advanced prompting methods, helping address challenges in Bengali NLP and bridging gaps in existing research.
3. Datasets
