5.2.6. Ablation studies
In this research, we conducted two types of ablation studies: (i) Ablation for Shot selection, and (ii) Ablation for temperature. Due to the pricing constraints of Closed-Source models and limited time, these ablation experiments were carried out using the open-source LLaMa model, specifically the LLaMa3 8B variant focusing on sentiment analysis tasks. The following paragraphs present the impact of these ablation studies.
(i) Ablation for shot selection.
Table 5 summarizes the performance of the LLaMA3 8B model across four different shot selections for sentiment classification tasks. The results demonstrate that the model’s performance is significantly affected by the number of shots, especially for Bengali sentiment analysis. As the shot count increases from zero to three, the model shows gradual improvement, indicating that providing a few examples helps it acquire prior knowledge and contextual understanding. However, in the four-shot scenario, performance drops, likely due to the following factors:
•
Alignment and Quality of Examples: The downstream performance of LLMs in Few-Shot scenarios heavily depends on the quality and alignment of the provided examples. In the four-shot scenario, the selected examples may have introduced conflicting signals or lacked alignment with the task, leading to reduced performance.
•
Exceeding Maximum Token Length: Including four examples could have exceeded the model’s maximum token length due to the larger number of tokens. Truncation may have caused a loss of important contextual information, impairing the model’s ability to fully comprehend the task-specific input.
•
Out-of-Distribution Examples: The examples used in the four-shot scenario might not represent the broader task distribution, introducing out-of-distribution issues. This misalignment could create gaps in contextual understanding and negatively affect performance.
This trend illustrates the importance of carefully curating few-shot examples to maximize performance, as more examples do not always lead to better outcomes, especially when the examples introduce noise or ambiguity.
(ii) Ablation for temperature.
The temperature parameter in LLMs significantly influences during the text generation. Higher temperature values allow for more diverse and creative outputs, introducing greater variability, while lower temperature values produce more focused and deterministic responses. Table 6 summarizes the impact of various temperature settings on the performance of the LLaMA3 8B model using three-shot prompting. Table 6 shows that lower temperature values (0.0–0.2) yield optimal results by producing focused and consistent outputs. In contrast, higher temperature values introduce excessive randomness, impairing the model’s predictive accuracy. As the temperature increases, the performance drops significantly, with accuracy plummeting from 94.87% at 0.1 to 10.90% at 2.
