6. Discussion
This research evaluates Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought prompting across three closed-source LLMs and three open-source LLMs. The following sections highlight the key findings and limitations.
•
Few-Shot prompting in the closed-source GPT-4o model outperformed other models in news classification, summarization, and question answering tasks (i.e., Details in Table 1, Table 3, Table 4). In contrast, CoT prompting with GPT-4o yielded superior performance in sentiment analysis. Overall, GPT-4o surpassed the remaining five models. However, Zero-Shot and CoT prompting underperformed in certain cases due to a lack of prior knowledge and limited reasoning capabilities.
•
In terms of reasoning capability, the closed-source LLMs, i.e., GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, and Gemini outperformed all open-source models. This is evident in mathematical and physics-related question-answering tasks, as illustrated in Fig. 3 of 3(b).
•
The ablation studies reveal that low-resource NLP downstream tasks are highly dependent on temperature values and the optimal number of shots, as shown in Section 5.2.6, Table 5, Table 6. Higher temperatures increase the model’s randomness and hallucination rate, reducing output reliability. Additionally, the number of shots significantly influences the text generation process, as highlighted in Table 5.
6.0.1. Limitations
Although this study evaluated four Bengali downstream tasks using three prompting techniques across six LLM models and conducted ablation experiments, some limitations still remain.
Monolingual: These experiments were conducted exclusively in Bengali, so the key findings are specific to the Bengali language.
Generalization: The optimal temperature and number of shots for LLM models are highly dependent on the specific dataset and tasks, making the optimal values identified in this research not universally generalizable.
Hallucination: During our experiments, we also tested other open-source large language models, including Mistral-Instruct-Bangla,8 Mistral-7B-v0.1,9 Mistral-7B-v0.3,10 (Jiang et al., 2023) Llama 2 7B Chat Touvron et al. (2023), etc. However, these models generated a high number of incorrect and hallucinated responses, making them unsuitable for our evaluation. Even with the chosen models, hallucination was a recurring issue, as they often deviated from a consistent response format. To address this, we applied heuristics, such as searching for correct answers within the string response, to evaluate the models effectively. Additionally, we faced some challenges with prompt length as Bangla language characters required more tokens per character for the models. This led to longer chain-of-thought prompts, which further increased hallucination and resulted in poorer performance for this prompting method. However, this study did not address or mitigate one of the most critical issues of LLMs, i.e., hallucination specifically for the low-resource Bengali language.
Dataset Size: The datasets used in this research contain a limited number of samples due to GPU support constraints. Another challenge we faced was, the gemini model encountered issues with its safety filter, which blocked certain answers, preventing us from evaluating those specific samples.
7. Conclusion and future works
In this study, we evaluated six LLM models, i.e., GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, Aya 101, BLOOM, LLaMA, and Gemini using three prompting techniques: Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and Chain-of-Thought. The evaluation focused on four low-resource Bengali downstream tasks: news classification, sentiment analysis, question answering, and summarization. Among the six models, three were open-source (LLaMA, Aya 101, and BLOOM), while the other three were closed-source (GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, and Gemini). The results indicate that GPT-4o performed the best overall, especially in tasks requiring a deep understanding of context and language nuances. However, open-source models like LLaMA also showed competitive performance on certain tasks, suggesting that open models can be effective for Bengali NLP, particularly when fine-tuned on complex and diverse Bengali datasets. We found that three-shot prompting generally produced better results than zero-shot, as it helped the models adapt more effectively to Bengali through examples. While Chain-of-Thought prompting was beneficial in some cases, it often led to reduced accuracy due to hallucination issues, especially with complex or longer prompts.
Although this research is the first to use three prompting techniques on four Bengali datasets with six LLM models, future work will address the identified limitations by expanding the scope beyond Bengali to include other low-resource and high-resource languages, enabling cross-lingual comparisons and improved generalization. Adaptive strategies for dynamically selecting temperature values and shot numbers can improve performance across tasks and datasets. Addressing hallucination issues will require techniques such as fact-checking modules, reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), and hybrid models that integrate knowledge graphs. To overcome dataset and computational constraints, larger datasets and collaborations with cloud providers or research institutions could be explored. Additionally, fine-tuning open-source models like LLaMA and BLOOM on high-quality Bengali datasets and domain-specific corpora can enhance their effectiveness, closing the performance gap with closed-source models and improving their applicability to low-resource NLP.
